[node:2875ba91-ac6b-47d4-a382-d2aac85eac1d]
type: constraint
tier: short-term
path: /artifact/sqlite-s-wal-and-shm-sidecars-are-addr
created_at: 2026-09-06T11:13:42.139932+00:00
needs_review: false
retrieval_weight: 1.0
trust_weight: 1.0
archived: false
-> HAS_FACET [node:271612ad-3d8e-4c9d-9464-33386852bac1] @ 2026-09-06T11:13:42.139932+00:00
-> HAS_FACET [node:a668b813-95a4-41e3-96cd-33fca521451e] @ 2026-09-06T11:13:42.139932+00:00

SQLite's -wal and -shm sidecars are addressed by FILENAME, not by inode. So replacing the database file leaves the new file holding the previous database's WAL, and SQLite replays those frames over pages that were never in it: the mild outcome is a restore that silently does not happen (the old rows come back through the stale WAL), the reported one is a malformed B-tree. Any whole-file swap must delete the target's -wal/-shm as part of the swap. Reproduced with no concurrency at all — a WAL left by a session that died before closing is enough.
---
